<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.8"/>
    <code code="51848-0" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation note"/>
    <title>Assessment</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item>Asymptomatic stenosis of the right internal carotid artery, 50 to 69 percent (I65.21). Atherosclerotic, moderate and asymptomatic: he has had no stroke, transient ischemic attack or transient monocular blindness, and the examination is normal. The bruit led to the duplex, which is evaluation of a sign rather than population screening. The annual stroke risk with asymptomatic moderate stenosis on modern medical therapy is low, about 1 percent a year or less. Endarterectomy is considered for asymptomatic stenosis of 70 to 99 percent in suitable patients when the surgical stroke and death risk is below 3 percent, so revascularization is not indicated at 50 to 69 percent. Best medical therapy is the treatment. Brain imaging would not change management now. [S]</item><item>Stage 2 hypertension (I10). Confirmed by home readings. Lowering blood pressure is safe with a stenosis of this degree and is the most effective single measure against stroke; the target is below 130/80. [S]</item><item>Lipids. LDL 148 mg/dL with carotid atherosclerosis: high-intensity statin therapy is indicated, aiming for at least a 50 percent reduction and an LDL below 70 mg/dL. [S]</item></list><paragraph>Data reviewed: Dr. Okafor-Brandt&apos;s intake note, ECG and blood tests: LDL cholesterol 148 mg/dL, HDL 46, triglycerides 170, HbA1c 5.5%, creatinine 0.98 mg/dL, eGFR 86. Home blood pressure average 154/90. Carotid duplex ultrasound this morning: right internal carotid artery peak systolic velocity 165 cm/s, end-diastolic velocity 38 cm/s, internal-to-common carotid ratio 2.6, with heterogeneous plaque at the bulb, consistent with 50 to 69 percent stenosis by consensus velocity criteria; left internal carotid peak systolic velocity 96 cm/s with less than 50 percent plaque; antegrade flow in both vertebral arteries. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.10" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="18776-5" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Plan of care note"/>
    <title>Plan of Treatment</title>
    <text><list><item>No endarterectomy or stenting; best medical therapy. [S]</item><item>Atorvastatin 40 mg daily, with a lipid panel and ALT in 4 to 12 weeks; aim for at least a 50 percent LDL reduction and LDL below 70 mg/dL. [S]</item><item>Aspirin 81 mg daily, avoiding NSAIDs. [S]</item><item>Blood pressure below 130/80 mmHg with the two drugs Dr. Okafor-Brandt is starting today. [S]</item><item>No brain imaging now. [S]</item><item>Repeat carotid duplex in twelve months, or at once with any symptoms; referral back if stenosis reaches 70 percent or more. [S]</item><item>Teach the BE FAST warning signs (balance, eyes, face, arm, speech, time) and call 911 at once, even if symptoms pass. [S]</item><item>Discussed with Dr. Okafor-Brandt, who saw him the same day. The results were explained to Mr. Cook, who chose medical therapy and repeated back the warning signs. [S]</item><item>Follow-up: Vascular neurology review after the repeat duplex in twelve months, or sooner with symptoms or progression. [S]</item></list></text>

  </section>
